00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:08.650
On March 11, 2020, the
World Health Organization

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:10.876
declared COVID-19 a pandemic.

00:00:10.876 --> 00:00:15.876
- WHO has been assessing this
outbreak around the globe.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.870
COVID-19 can be
characterized as a pandemic.

00:00:20.870 --> 00:00:23.090
- This virus is not
only making people sick,

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.220
but it's upending our daily lives.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.340
That includes canceling vacations,

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:30.060
postponing events, and
upending employment.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:32.850
Did you plan a wedding that
now needs to be postponed?

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.030
Did you have to cancel
your European vacation

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:36.340
after the travel ban?

00:00:36.340 --> 00:00:39.540
Is your small business
suddenly behind on rent?

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.640
Well, today we're gonna
talk about how you might

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.100
be able to use contract law
and insurance agreements

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.200
to get refunds and even relief.

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.090
Let me start with a disclaimer

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.410
that probably goes without saying,

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.670
but I'm going to say it
because I'm a lawyer.

00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.980
Nothing in this video should
be construed as legal advice.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:54.860
Nothing I say creates an
attorney-client relationship

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:56.230
with you, the viewer.

00:00:56.230 --> 00:00:58.593
If you have a
coronavirus-related question,

00:00:58.593 --> 00:01:01.030
you should contact your own lawyer,

00:01:01.030 --> 00:01:03.450
or better yet, your doctor.

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:04.340
Now, with that out of the way,

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:06.270
I do want to give you one more warning.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.810
What I'm going tell you
is incredibly powerful.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:11.220
But I want you to use
this information wisely.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.770
Let's say you had a wedding
scheduled for April,

00:01:13.770 --> 00:01:15.740
and maybe that won't be happening now.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:18.460
So naturally you're gonna want
to cancel with the caterers,

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:19.840
the band, and the photographer.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.000
But remember, those are people too.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.950
That's how they make their living,

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.390
and it's going to be hard
times ahead for those people.

00:01:26.390 --> 00:01:28.560
Everyone in the service
industry is going to suffer.

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:30.280
A photographer can't work from home.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.610
No one's going to be
hiring bands for parties

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.120
that won't be happening
for the foreseeable future.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:36.520
Unless you live in Florida.

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.010
But it might be the
same with your landlord.

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:41.220
Sure, your apartment
building might be part

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.450
of a giant conglomerate
owned by BlackRock,

00:01:43.450 --> 00:01:47.280
but it also might be the only
building owned by a family.

00:01:47.280 --> 00:01:49.840
There are always downstream consequences.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.990
So, sure, no one's going to blame you

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:53.540
for having to look out for yourself.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:56.810
But remember that your
actions effect others too,

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:58.410
and everyone is going to be struggling

00:01:58.410 --> 00:01:59.810
for the foreseeable future.

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:01.590
So with that business out of the way,

00:02:01.590 --> 00:02:04.550
today we're going to be
covering such sexy legal topics

00:02:04.550 --> 00:02:07.485
as terms and conditions, force majeure,

00:02:07.485 --> 00:02:10.640
and insurance riders.

00:02:10.640 --> 00:02:11.802
Oh yeah.

00:02:11.802 --> 00:02:14.719
(triumphant music)

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:18.070
So let's start with the basics.

00:02:18.070 --> 00:02:20.750
When you make a reservation
when you buy plane tickets,

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.470
or when you purchase
anything on the internet,

00:02:22.470 --> 00:02:25.440
you agree to abide by
certain terms and conditions.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:26.800
You can find out what you agree to

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:28.410
by clicking on the terms
and conditions page

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.930
of most websites or
reviewing whatever document

00:02:30.930 --> 00:02:32.990
they send you by email or snail mail.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:34.920
This document contains all of the legalese

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.118
that you didn't review and
merely glanced at, at best.

00:02:37.118 --> 00:02:40.778
And unfortunately, this is
generally a binding contract

00:02:40.778 --> 00:02:42.050
that applies to you.

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:43.810
The good news is that
you can learn to think

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:45.700
like a lawyer to help
yourself out of a jam,

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:48.620
often using the exact same
language that you find

00:02:48.620 --> 00:02:52.030
in those pages of text that
you absolutely did not read.

00:02:52.030 --> 00:02:53.150
Don't try and fool anyone.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:53.983
You didn't read it.

00:02:53.983 --> 00:02:55.450
Now, if you made travel reservations,

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:57.640
you should immediately review
the terms and conditions pages

00:02:57.640 --> 00:02:59.260
to see what terms applied originally,

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:01.790
and whether that company
has updated their policies

00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:03.640
because of the coronavirus.

00:03:03.640 --> 00:03:06.770
For example, Airbnb is the
original travel disrupter

00:03:06.770 --> 00:03:08.250
and they disrupted the travel industry

00:03:08.250 --> 00:03:09.880
by creating a website
that lets people rent out

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.120
their homes and extra rooms to travelers.

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:14.240
Now, generally Airbnb allows their hosts

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:16.460
to set their own cancellation policy.

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:17.930
Some hosts require a deposit

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:20.040
and more than 48 hours notice to cancel.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.750
Other hosts are more strict,
taking the guest deposit

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.580
if the person cancels for any reason.

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:27.760
But that changed on March
14 when Airbnb notified

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:29.280
hosts and guests that it was updating

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:31.500
their extenuating circumstances policy.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:34.500
The site now allows all guests
to cancel all reservations

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.270
without penalty anywhere in the world

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.200
as long as the reservation for the travel

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:40.750
was between March 14 and April 14.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:43.200
And as things go on and
potentially get worse,

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.620
that policy may be updated again.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:48.090
A lot of hosts are very
unhappy with this result,

00:03:48.090 --> 00:03:50.120
since many depend on the
income to afford rent

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.290
or pay for their mortgage.

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:53.120
But if you're a guest,
however, you might be able

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:55.570
to cancel your reservation
and monitor the host

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:58.700
to ensure that they comply
with Airbnb's new policy.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:00.280
It's really important not only to check

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:03.120
the original terms, but also
if anything has been updated.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.400
Business Insider has
compiled a list of hotels

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:07.170
which have updated their
cancellation policies,

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:10.160
and I'll put the link to that
down in the description below.

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:13.210
Many hotels run by groups
like Disney, Hilton, and Hyatt

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:14.830
are waiving cancellation fees for travel

00:04:14.830 --> 00:04:15.663
booked through April,

00:04:15.663 --> 00:04:17.700
and if you have travel dates
that are later in the year

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:20.040
you might want to jump
on the phone immediately.

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:22.110
Because you know things are
bad when travel companies

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.970
are voluntarily relaxing
their contractual terms.

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.918
If the customer service
people are giving you

00:04:27.918 --> 00:04:29.170
a hard time one pro tip is to use

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:31.350
the government warnings to your advantage.

00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:34.080
For example, the WHO, the
World Health Organization,

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:35.770
declared coronavirus a pandemic,

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:38.680
and experts are unsure for
how long extreme measures

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:42.100
like social distancing, isolation,
and quarantine will last.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:44.680
The State Department has
issued travel advisories

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:46.660
and warnings for certain countries.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:48.770
State governors have prohibited gatherings

00:04:48.770 --> 00:04:51.640
and are locking down
huge swaths of people,

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:53.430
and when you're talking
to customer service

00:04:53.430 --> 00:04:55.650
you can site these
warnings as evidence to why

00:04:55.650 --> 00:04:58.500
you cannot travel and should get a refund.

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:01.620
We'll go into detail about
these specific legal doctrines

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.980
that you might use to bootstrap
some of these warnings

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:06.280
in dealing with customer
service or other companies

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:08.380
that you might have to cancel
in order to get a refund.

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:10.900
Now, not all travel or
commercial companies

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:13.070
are going to be as gracious as the ones

00:05:13.070 --> 00:05:14.550
that are voluntarily rolling back

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:16.240
their cancellation policies.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:18.916
But if you dig into the
terms and conditions

00:05:18.916 --> 00:05:21.917
you might find specific
language or fine print

00:05:21.917 --> 00:05:24.317
that says something along the lines of

00:05:24.317 --> 00:05:26.927
"the company is not
responsible for any injury,

00:05:26.927 --> 00:05:29.887
"loss, or damage to
person or property, death,

00:05:29.887 --> 00:05:32.527
"delay, or inconvenience
in connection with

00:05:32.527 --> 00:05:34.927
"the provision of any goods
or services occasioned

00:05:34.927 --> 00:05:37.800
"by or resulting from,
but not limited to,"

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:39.067
and this is the important part,

00:05:39.067 --> 00:05:42.279
"acts of God or force majeure."

00:05:42.279 --> 00:05:46.020
That brings us to one of the
most important legal doctrines

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:47.710
that you're probably
going to be dealing with

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:50.180
in this particular situation.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.560
Acts of God and force majeure.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:55.340
Now, force majeure is
basically like a magic spell

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:57.250
used by dark wizards called lawyers.

00:05:57.250 --> 00:05:58.096
Force majeure!

00:05:58.096 --> 00:05:58.929
(exciting music)

00:05:58.929 --> 00:05:59.762
I'm just kidding.

00:05:59.762 --> 00:06:02.540
It's a very dry contractual
provision in most circumstances,

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:06.480
but in circumstances like these
it can be just as powerful

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.980
because most contracts include
a force majeure clause.

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:12.320
These clauses excuse a
party from performing

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:14.930
its obligations under the
contract if they are faced

00:06:14.930 --> 00:06:18.150
with circumstances beyond
that party's control.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.890
The phrase force majeure is
French for superior force.

00:06:21.890 --> 00:06:24.100
The kind of superior
force that would interfere

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:26.580
with the performance of a contract,

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:29.080
and that includes wars, labor disputes,

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:30.620
and extreme weather events.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:32.560
Generally, the test for whether something

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:35.440
is a force majeure event
requires three specific elements.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:37.680
Number one, the element
is beyond the control

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:38.840
of the affected party.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:40.870
Two, the affected party's
ability to perform

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:42.750
its part of the contract
must have been prevented,

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:44.913
impeded, or hindered by the event.

00:06:44.913 --> 00:06:47.460
Three, the affected party must have taken

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.610
all reasonable steps to avoid or mitigate

00:06:49.610 --> 00:06:51.650
the event or its consequences.

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:54.520
There are two categories
of force majeure events.

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:56.760
The first category is
for acts of government

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:59.020
that change the legal
or political environment

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:01.510
that could impact a project or a business.

00:07:01.510 --> 00:07:03.940
This could include things
like war, terrorism,

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:05.410
labor strikes, or riots.

00:07:05.410 --> 00:07:07.710
The second category
includes natural disasters,

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:10.050
such as floods, earthquakes, hurricanes,

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:11.740
and other weather disturbances that are

00:07:11.740 --> 00:07:13.740
sometimes called acts of God.

00:07:13.740 --> 00:07:16.460
When a party is affected
by a force majeure

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:19.570
they are usually relieved
from performing the contract.

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:21.730
There may be disputes
about whether that persons

00:07:21.730 --> 00:07:24.820
contractual obligations are
canceled or just postponed.

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:27.200
Now, there's no general
statute that defines

00:07:27.200 --> 00:07:29.350
what constitutes a force majeure event.

00:07:29.350 --> 00:07:32.670
A parties ability to claim
relief for a force majeure event

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.000
depends on the way that
the contractual terms

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:37.890
are written and the context
of the rest of the contract.

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:40.350
Most contracts contain a
detailed list of events

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:41.880
that are deemed to be force majeure

00:07:41.880 --> 00:07:43.270
and beyond the parties control.

00:07:43.270 --> 00:07:46.100
Some contracts include
things like diseases,

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:48.250
pandemics, or epidemics in their list of

00:07:48.250 --> 00:07:51.420
naturally occurring events
that may disrupt the contract.

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:54.400
However, if a contract
clause omits language

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:55.810
about diseases and pandemics,

00:07:55.810 --> 00:07:57.530
then there will probably be a dispute

00:07:57.530 --> 00:08:00.310
about whether a pandemic,
like coronavirus,

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:03.240
is included in a force majeure event.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:06.160
For example, if the contract
lists a bunch of categories

00:08:06.160 --> 00:08:08.410
that do constitute a force majeure,

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:11.100
then the fact that a
pandemic is not listed in it

00:08:11.100 --> 00:08:13.740
could be evidence that a
pandemic is not covered.

00:08:13.740 --> 00:08:15.880
If there is vague language that says

00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:17.870
that a force majeure can be anything

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:21.200
like an example of the
things that are given,

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:22.730
then you have a better argument.

00:08:22.730 --> 00:08:24.860
But there's no federal law that says

00:08:24.860 --> 00:08:27.810
these particular things are
considered force majeure.

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:30.260
But if you are looking
through your own contract

00:08:30.260 --> 00:08:31.860
or your own terms and conditions,

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:34.500
here's a couple examples of
what force majeure clauses

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:36.980
look like so you'll know what to look for.

00:08:36.980 --> 00:08:39.450
Notwithstanding anything to
the contrary contained herein,

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:41.990
neither party shall be liable
for any delays or failures

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:43.890
in performance resulting from acts beyond

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:46.830
its reasonable control
including, without limitation,

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:49.100
acts of God, acts of war or terrorism,

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:51.860
shortage of supply,
breakdowns or malfunctions,

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:54.470
interruptions or malfunction
of computer facilities,

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:56.130
or loss of data due to power failures

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:58.530
or mechanical difficulties
with information storage

00:08:58.530 --> 00:09:01.520
or retrieval systems, labor
difficulties or civil unrest.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:02.580
Notwithstanding the foregoing,

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:03.850
in the event of such an occurrence,

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:06.070
each party agrees to
make a good faith effort

00:09:06.070 --> 00:09:08.401
to perform its obligations hereunder.

00:09:08.401 --> 00:09:11.160
So would coronavirus be
a force majeure event

00:09:11.160 --> 00:09:12.220
under this clause?

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:14.880
Well, this is why lawyers
are going to outlive everyone

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:16.490
except the cockroaches.

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:19.130
Because lawyers will argue
that the acts of God language

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:21.720
operates as a catchall,
with respect to events

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:24.870
which are beyond the control
of the parties to the contract.

00:09:24.870 --> 00:09:27.710
Since a pandemic would be
beyond the reasonable control

00:09:27.710 --> 00:09:30.280
of the parties, it seems
like the coronavirus crisis

00:09:30.280 --> 00:09:33.360
would be a force majeure event
under this type of clause.

00:09:33.360 --> 00:09:36.560
But you can also argue that
since it's not mentioned there,

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:38.530
perhaps it doesn't cover that.

00:09:38.530 --> 00:09:42.420
Or that the actual loss
of business is not related

00:09:42.420 --> 00:09:45.430
to the coronavirus per
se, but because of acts

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:48.850
of other people who are not
going to various businesses.

00:09:48.850 --> 00:09:51.180
You can go either way
on some of these things.

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:53.930
But statements like the
WHO declaring coronavirus

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:56.090
a pandemic is certainly
helpful to consumers

00:09:56.090 --> 00:09:57.030
who want to make their case,

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:59.530
as are the statements of
various state governors

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:02.739
who are putting entire states on lockdown.

00:10:02.739 --> 00:10:04.750
So in a situation like this,

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:07.950
you can argue that this is
a change in circumstances

00:10:07.950 --> 00:10:11.050
of the law that makes
performance impossible,

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:13.990
and you can also argue
that it is a natural event,

00:10:13.990 --> 00:10:17.188
like a plague or some other weather event,

00:10:17.188 --> 00:10:20.520
such as a natural disaster
that would also be covered

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:21.740
by a force majeure clause.

00:10:21.740 --> 00:10:23.810
There are arguments for both sides.

00:10:23.810 --> 00:10:26.360
Keep in mind that making this
argument of a force majeure

00:10:26.360 --> 00:10:28.810
event doesn't necessarily
mean that the party themselves

00:10:28.810 --> 00:10:31.100
needs to test positive for coronavirus.

00:10:31.100 --> 00:10:33.020
What we're looking for
here are disruptions

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:35.330
that are the direct result of coronavirus,

00:10:35.330 --> 00:10:38.630
like a forced self-isolation
or shutting down a factory.

00:10:38.630 --> 00:10:40.560
These are the consequences that impact

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:43.690
a parties ability to fulfill
its contractual obligations.

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:45.530
So for example, think about
all the vendors that were

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:48.760
providing services to
Miami's Ultra Music Festival.

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:51.090
Despite the festival
taking place in Florida,

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:54.880
where apparently anything
goes and there are no laws,

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:56.320
the festival was actually canceled

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:58.730
after Florida's governor
declared a state of emergency.

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:01.547
- That allows us to create
a unified command structure.

00:11:01.547 --> 00:11:05.417
It also allows, if need be,
out of state medical personnel

00:11:05.417 --> 00:11:07.200
to operate in Florida.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:09.140
- Since the event was
canceled due to events

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:10.970
beyond their control, most
vendors should be able

00:11:10.970 --> 00:11:13.340
to rely on their contract's
force majeure language

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:16.557
to justify not performing the
services under the contract.

00:11:16.557 --> 00:11:18.380
If money changed hands,

00:11:18.380 --> 00:11:21.340
I would expect that that
money would change hands again

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:23.600
and go back to the original buyer.

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:25.000
But this isn't a silver bullet.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:26.360
It doesn't work all the time.

00:11:26.360 --> 00:11:28.800
For example, if we're talking
about financial agreements,

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:31.310
like an investor's
contract to fund a startup,

00:11:31.310 --> 00:11:33.465
a force majeure event
may not excuse investors

00:11:33.465 --> 00:11:35.820
from making payments
that they agreed to make

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:36.770
under a contract.

00:11:36.770 --> 00:11:39.630
This is what happened in
the 2008 financial meltdown,

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:41.430
which resulted in many
lawsuits about whether

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:44.180
the financial crisis
was a qualifying event

00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:45.750
for force majeure purposes.

00:11:45.750 --> 00:11:48.680
Most courts interpreted force
majeure clauses very narrowly.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:51.360
Market forces didn't
count as a force majeure

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:53.260
unless the specific financial scenario

00:11:53.260 --> 00:11:54.890
was spelled out in the clause.

00:11:54.890 --> 00:11:56.730
Some people also unhappily discovered

00:11:56.730 --> 00:11:59.160
that market forces were directly excluded

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:01.800
by the contract as an
excuse for non-performance.

00:12:01.800 --> 00:12:04.370
That surely resulted in a
lot of angry phone calls

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:08.450
to the parties lawyers,
which keeps us employed too.

00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:10.170
But force majeure clauses are not

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:11.760
a get out of jail free card.

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:13.930
They don't normally
operate to excuse a party

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:16.200
from making rent payments,
operating expenses,

00:12:16.200 --> 00:12:17.460
or loan payments.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:19.570
But in a situation like this,

00:12:19.570 --> 00:12:23.100
with a once in a century
pandemic, all bets are off.

00:12:23.100 --> 00:12:25.990
Lawyers are going to be
arguing over these clauses

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:29.210
until we die, which for some of us,

00:12:29.210 --> 00:12:31.680
may be pretty soon.

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:35.080
But force majeure is not
the only argument available

00:12:35.080 --> 00:12:38.160
to consumers and people
that are trying to get out

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:40.250
of a contract or not pay something.

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:42.410
If a pandemic isn't
included in the contract

00:12:42.410 --> 00:12:44.570
or isn't interpreted
to be in the contract,

00:12:44.570 --> 00:12:47.260
then the parties might have
to rely on other defenses

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:49.010
that excuse them from performing.

00:12:49.010 --> 00:12:51.600
Under American law, the
defenses of frustration

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:53.700
and impossibility may apply.

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:56.100
These defenses are usually
interpreted narrowly,

00:12:56.100 --> 00:12:58.010
and they usually require
two considerations.

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:00.620
Number one, whether the
subject matter of the contract

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:02.780
or the means of performance
has been destroyed

00:13:02.780 --> 00:13:06.358
such that performance is
rendered objectively impossible.

00:13:06.358 --> 00:13:09.390
Two, whether the central
purpose of the contract

00:13:09.390 --> 00:13:11.880
has been frustrated or
the contract has become

00:13:11.880 --> 00:13:13.810
radically different from
what was contemplated

00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:16.770
by the parties at the time
they agreed on the terms.

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:18.210
Many US states have adopted

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:20.600
the Uniform Commercial Code, or UCC.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:24.330
The UCC applies when you buy
a good generally speaking.

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:27.987
The UCC includes an excuse
where performance has been made

00:13:27.987 --> 00:13:30.826
"impracticable by the
occurrence of an event,"

00:13:30.826 --> 00:13:33.937
"the non-occurrence of
which was a basic assumption

00:13:33.937 --> 00:13:35.720
"on which the contract was made."

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:38.300
So sorry for the double
negative, but in other words,

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:41.680
something that the contract
assumed wouldn't happen.

00:13:41.680 --> 00:13:43.690
This defense might work
in a scenario where

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:45.310
a state governor has canceled an event

00:13:45.310 --> 00:13:48.590
because it would be attended
by more than 50 people.

00:13:48.590 --> 00:13:50.600
If the event that needed to occur

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:52.390
was a gathering of thousands of people,

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:54.400
such as those attending
the music festival,

00:13:54.400 --> 00:13:57.610
then arguably the inability
to gather in a group

00:13:57.610 --> 00:14:00.230
because it was made illegal could justify

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:02.270
non-performance as impracticable,

00:14:02.270 --> 00:14:06.060
which takes me to the
sexiest legal topic of all.

00:14:06.060 --> 00:14:10.100
I saved the best for
last, insurance policies.

00:14:10.100 --> 00:14:12.560
Now, if a party to a contract
can't meet its obligations

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:14.130
due to the coronavirus outbreak,

00:14:14.130 --> 00:14:16.340
you probably need to find out if insurance

00:14:16.340 --> 00:14:17.530
will cover you losses.

00:14:17.530 --> 00:14:18.800
Sometimes it will.

00:14:18.800 --> 00:14:20.560
Many commercial property
insurance policies

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:23.200
provide business interruption coverage.

00:14:23.200 --> 00:14:25.010
But companies covered by these policies

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:26.820
won't be able to invoke
them unless they show

00:14:26.820 --> 00:14:29.450
direct physical loss to
property of the insured,

00:14:29.450 --> 00:14:31.490
its customers, or its suppliers.

00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:34.210
Commercial insurers also
sell specific products

00:14:34.210 --> 00:14:36.700
dealing with force majeure,
political risk insurance,

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:38.430
or trade disruption insurance.

00:14:38.430 --> 00:14:42.530
Sometimes a policy that would
otherwise cover these things

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:43.880
have what's called a rider,

00:14:43.880 --> 00:14:47.620
which excludes the specific
occurrence of things.

00:14:47.620 --> 00:14:50.470
You'll often see a terrorism rider,

00:14:50.470 --> 00:14:53.739
that if your store was
bombed by a terrorist

00:14:53.739 --> 00:14:58.040
then the insurance doesn't
apply in that particular case.

00:14:58.040 --> 00:15:00.200
So you have to check not
only the policy itself,

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:02.240
but the riders that go
along with the policy.

00:15:02.240 --> 00:15:04.119
If you're an individual,

00:15:04.119 --> 00:15:04.960
you might check your homeowners insurance,

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.530
you might check your renters insurance.

00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:09.040
Hell, you might try your car insurance.

00:15:09.040 --> 00:15:10.990
Because consumers also have insurance

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:12.000
implications to consider.

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.330
Many travelers buy travel
insurance to protect them

00:15:14.330 --> 00:15:16.970
from things like a deadly
pandemic that restricts travel.

00:15:16.970 --> 00:15:18.910
But if you think that
your insurance company

00:15:18.910 --> 00:15:21.240
does have you covered
you may be disappointed,

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:23.630
because insurance companies are terrible.

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:25.500
Once again, you have to
look to the fine print,

00:15:25.500 --> 00:15:29.240
and this is, frankly,
just always good advice.

00:15:29.240 --> 00:15:31.670
Many travel insurance companies
exclude health epidemics

00:15:31.670 --> 00:15:33.660
or pandemics in their standard policies.

00:15:33.660 --> 00:15:35.890
They won't even refund
travelers who are afraid

00:15:35.890 --> 00:15:37.840
to travel because of the virus.

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:38.960
If you plan to go later in the year,

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:41.070
you might buy a policy that has a cancel

00:15:41.070 --> 00:15:42.800
for any reason clause.

00:15:42.800 --> 00:15:45.110
However, some insurance
companies are already limiting

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:47.450
the number of cancel
for any reason policies

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:49.370
that they're going to
sell in this environment.

00:15:49.370 --> 00:15:51.330
Now, that is largely the bad news.

00:15:51.330 --> 00:15:53.190
But let me give you some good news.

00:15:53.190 --> 00:15:57.070
It looks like help for small
businesses is on the way.

00:15:57.070 --> 00:16:00.050
It's going to depend on what
federal relief is passed

00:16:00.050 --> 00:16:03.390
by congress and state laws
will vary state by state.

00:16:03.390 --> 00:16:05.980
But everyone is on board
to try and help out

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:08.120
as many people as possible.

00:16:08.120 --> 00:16:10.380
Now, I can't go into
detail about all of these

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:14.330
different relief laws, mainly
because they don't yet exist.

00:16:14.330 --> 00:16:15.900
The debate is going on in congress.

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:18.210
But the good news is that it seems like

00:16:18.210 --> 00:16:19.820
everyone is on board with trying to help

00:16:19.820 --> 00:16:22.800
as many people for as long as possible.

00:16:22.800 --> 00:16:25.740
Because, really, we only recently realized

00:16:25.740 --> 00:16:29.630
how serious this whole
coronavirus thing is.

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:31.941
If you want to see my prior video on how

00:16:31.941 --> 00:16:35.020
the US government has botched its response

00:16:35.020 --> 00:16:36.590
to the coronavirus I'll leave a link

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:39.040
to the descriptions of my
prior video on the subject.

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:40.910
- No, I don't take responsibility at all.

00:16:40.910 --> 00:16:43.100
- But as you know, if
you own a small business,

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:45.380
the coronavirus is
probably already disrupting

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:47.800
your ability to stay open
and pay your employees.

00:16:47.800 --> 00:16:49.640
Many restaurants are expected to close

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:51.870
for the duration of the lockdown.

00:16:51.870 --> 00:16:54.190
Some states have shut down tattoo parlors,

00:16:54.190 --> 00:16:58.190
nail salons, restaurants,
and yes, even GameStop.

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:02.140
Any non-essential company
is being shut down.

00:17:02.140 --> 00:17:04.410
This means that many businesses
are going to struggle

00:17:04.410 --> 00:17:06.900
to survive and it's clear
that the federal government

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:09.670
is trying to get its act together
and trying to help people.

00:17:09.670 --> 00:17:12.640
It's just not clear when that
relief is going to happen.

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:14.840
- There's tremendous spirit
to get something done,

00:17:14.840 --> 00:17:15.890
so we'll see what happens.

00:17:15.890 --> 00:17:18.210
- So you might have to look to your state

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:19.330
and local governments for help.

00:17:19.330 --> 00:17:21.800
For example, New York
City Mayor, Bill De Blasio

00:17:21.800 --> 00:17:24.180
announced that the city is
offering no-interest loans

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:27.850
to businesses which see sales
decrease by 25% or more.

00:17:27.850 --> 00:17:31.690
To qualify, a business must
have fewer than 100 employees.

00:17:31.690 --> 00:17:33.710
For businesses with
less than five workers,

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:35.710
the city is offering grants to help cover

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:39.160
40% of payroll costs and
this hopefully will help

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:43.060
people stay employed during
the duration of this pandemic.

00:17:43.060 --> 00:17:45.340
Similarly, Seattle Mayor, Jenny Durkan,

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:47.600
will increase funds available
for micro-businesses

00:17:47.600 --> 00:17:50.290
to help small companies access SBA loans,

00:17:50.290 --> 00:17:53.060
and provide relief for utility payments.

00:17:53.060 --> 00:17:54.420
The city is also delaying collection

00:17:54.420 --> 00:17:56.720
of business and occupation taxes.

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:58.660
At the federal level,
we are starting to see

00:17:58.660 --> 00:18:00.771
mobilization of the administrative state.

00:18:00.771 --> 00:18:03.070
The Small Business Administration has

00:18:03.070 --> 00:18:05.220
an Economic Injury Disaster Loan Program

00:18:05.220 --> 00:18:06.440
that will give low-interest loans

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:08.100
to small businesses and non-profits

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:11.180
that have been severely
impacted by the coronavirus.

00:18:11.180 --> 00:18:13.540
Hopefully, this will just
be the tip of the iceberg.

00:18:13.540 --> 00:18:16.310
I expect a whole raft of new
and exciting statutory laws

00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:17.900
that will help people in this situation,

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:19.610
and all of the fun litigation

00:18:19.610 --> 00:18:21.410
that's going to come as a result of it.

00:18:21.410 --> 00:18:24.170
But, of course, this is a
constantly evolving situation.

00:18:24.170 --> 00:18:26.150
Although we're focused on
the next four to six weeks,

00:18:26.150 --> 00:18:27.830
the reality is that
quarantine and isolation

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:30.950
and self-isolation may last
for a lot longer than that.

00:18:30.950 --> 00:18:33.160
Of course, the longer
everyday life is disrupted,

00:18:33.160 --> 00:18:35.270
the greater economic impact of all

00:18:35.270 --> 00:18:37.130
the businesses and contracts.

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:38.930
But if there's one
take-away from all of this,

00:18:38.930 --> 00:18:41.030
it's that, as always,

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:43.470
it's the lawyers who are the real heroes

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:45.450
that are looking out for those in need.

00:18:45.450 --> 00:18:46.920
Eat it, doctors and nurses.

00:18:46.920 --> 00:18:50.480
Now, of course, part of what
got us here is a failure

00:18:50.480 --> 00:18:52.820
